// =============================================================================
// MemQL Action Reference (simplified -- construct-invocation ADR Decision 3)
// =============================================================================
//
// This file is the source of truth for the `action` syntax. It is NOT loaded
// by the engine (the leading underscore on the path tells every walker to skip
// it -- see core/dslfs/walker.go).
//
// `action` is the behavioral DSL's EXTERNAL-SIDE-EFFECT construct. From the
// behavioral-constructs ADR (docs/internal/design/dsl-behavioral-constructs-adr.md,
// §2 + §2.3):
//
//     logic decides, mutations persist, actions touch the world,
//     queries read, automations orchestrate and react.
//
// An action performs EXACTLY ONE external capability call on a surface
// (shell.* / fs.* / http.* / integration.* / mcp.*) and NEVER touches the
// MemQL graph. The dividing line is "what does it touch?": the outside world
// -> action; our graph -> mutation; read/compute -> query / logic. Because an
// action's effect is reproducible from its inputs, it can be replayed and
// fingerprint-verified token-free -- the foundation the action library and the
// deployment bundle are built on.
//
// =============================================================================
//   1. The simplified shape -- args + a single capability call
// =============================================================================
//
// An action is reduced to an `args { ... }` block plus a SINGLE
// `capability <verb>(...)` call (construct-invocation ADR Decision 3). There is
// NO `body { }` and no `return` -- the body IS the one capability call. The
// bare verb resolves through the file-top `use capabilities.<path>.{ verb }`
// import to a full dotted capability name.
//
//   @description("...")    Human-readable description. KEPT -- it is the
//                          planner's retrieval embedding source. The retired
//                          `intent` text merges into it.
//   args { <name> <type> [@required] ... }
//                          The action's input schema (same grammar as every
//                          other construct's args block). @description on an
//                          args FIELD is parsed and discarded (#2615) -- do
//                          not write it; per-field docs arrive with /// doc
//                          comments (#2601). The declaration-level
//                          @description above is the load-bearing one.
//   capability <verb>(<key>: <value>, ...)
//                          The single external capability. Each value is a
//                          LITERAL or an `args.<x>` reference -- never a call
//                          into another construct (an action is atomic).
//
// Compliant example -- the deployment bundle's clone primitive:
//
// Every capability verb an action calls is imported file-top: the parser
// accepts `use` only in the preamble, before the first declaration, so all
// three imports for the examples below (shell.script, fs.readFile,
// integration.github.tagRelease) live here together rather than beside each
// example.

use capabilities.shell.{ script }
use capabilities.fs.{ readFile }
use capabilities.integration.github.{ tagRelease }

@description("Check out a git repository at a specific ref on the deploy runner, so a build runs against a pinned version.")
action cloneRepoAtVersion {
  args {
    workdir string!
    ref     string!
  }
  capability script(script: "deploy.cloneRepo", workdir: args.workdir, ref: args.ref)
}

// A `shell.*` action runs a NAMED capability script (the I14 contract, #2221),
// NOT an arbitrary command string. The reserved `script` arg names the
// allowlisted capability-script id; every other arg is a structured param. The
// action executor's capability-script runner
// (component/automations/steps/capability_script.go) resolves the id to a
// scripts/* path, runs it with the params as `--name=value` argv flags (no
// shell, no `sh -c`), and parses the one JSON envelope it writes to stdout.
// This is injection-safe by construction -- an author/event value can only pick
// an allowlisted script and travels as data, never as a command line. (The raw
// `shell.exec` + interpolated-command form is RETIRED for exactly that reason
// -- it was a command-injection sink.)

// =============================================================================
//   2. Other capability namespaces -- fs / integration
// =============================================================================
//
// The capability namespace selects the backend; the shape is identical.
//
// 2a. A filesystem read (the capability's @sideEffect class = read). Its
//     `readFile` verb is imported in the file-top preamble above:

@description("Read a deployment manifest off the surface so a later step can inspect it.")
action readManifest {
  args {
    path string!
  }
  capability readFile(path: args.path)
}

// 2b. An integration-backed action. A SIDE-EFFECTING integration (e.g. tag a
//     release, post to Slack) is reachable ONLY through an action -- its
//     capability resolves to integration.* instead of shell.*/fs.* (ADR §3).
//     A read-only integration stays a builtin callable from queries/logic. The
//     authoritative side-effect class lives on the CAPABILITY declaration
//     (dsl/capabilities/capabilities.memql), never on the action. Its
//     `tagRelease` verb is imported in the file-top preamble above.

@description("Cut a git tag + release on GitHub for the version that just deployed.")
action tagReleaseExample {
  args {
    repo    string!
    version string!
  }
  capability tagRelease(repo: args.repo, tag: args.version)
}

// =============================================================================
//   3. Calling an action -- the automation step (ADR Decision 1+2)
// =============================================================================
//
// An action is invoked from an automation step in the kind-prefixed invocation
// form `action <name>(<colon-args>)`. The action resolves by NAME (no
// `@version` suffix). Step args bind to the action's `args`:
//
//   use deployment.actions.{ cloneRepoAtVersion }
//
//   automation deployEngine @trigger(event="deploy.requested.staging") {
//     step clone {
//       action cloneRepoAtVersion(workdir: "/work/memql", ref: event.payload.ref)
//     }
//     // ... next step is a mutation that records the result (decide->act->persist)
//   }
//
// An identical input replays token-free: binding the same args renders the
// same capability arguments, the capability runs on the default surface, and
// the result fingerprint (component/harness/actionreplay.Fingerprint)
// reproduces. A mismatch is what the surface-aware trust ladder escalates on.
//
// =============================================================================
//   4. Dropped legacy surfaces (rejected with migration-pointing errors)
// =============================================================================
//
//   @kind          composites are automations now; primitives need no marker.
//   @sideEffect    moved onto the CAPABILITY declaration (unspoofable, ADR §7).
//   @reliability   machine-managed runtime state, not source.
//   intent         merged into @description.
//   params         renamed to args.
//   argTemplate    replaced by the typed capability <verb>(...) call;
//   $params.X      the string-template form is retired.
//   capability "<verb>"   the stringly form is retired -- write `capability
//                         <verb>(...)` and import the verb.
//   body { }       an action has no procedural body (ADR Decision 5).
//
// =============================================================================
//   5. NON-COMPLIANT examples (rejected by the I7 call-graph validator)
// =============================================================================
//
// 5a. An action that computes/calls another construct. An action is atomic: a
//     capability arg may not be a logic / query / mutation call.
//
//   // INVALID: a capability arg calls a logic.
//   action deployBad {
//     args { ref string @required }
//     capability script(cmd: nextSemver(args.ref))   // <- calls logic: rejected
//   }
//
// 5b. An action that touches the graph. Graph writes stay mutations.
//
//   // INVALID: an action may not perform a graph write -- that is a mutation.
//   action recordBad {
//     args { id string @required }
//     capability script(script: "x")
//     insert { id: args.id }                          // <- graph write: rejected
//   }
//
// 5c. An action declaring more than one capability call.
//
//   // INVALID: two capability calls in one action.
//   action twoThingsBad {
//     args { }
//     capability script(script: "x")
//     capability writeFile(path: "y")                 // <- second capability: rejected
//   }
//
// 5d. An action carrying a trigger. Reactivity is automations-only (ADR §2.4).
//
//   // INVALID: only automations may carry @trigger.
//   @trigger(event="deploy.requested")                // <- rejected on an action
//   action onDeployBad { args { } capability script(script: "x") }
//
// See `_automation.memql` for the construct that orchestrates actions, and
// `_logic.memql` for the pure-decision construct an action's inputs come from.
// =============================================================================
